                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_tacit:         `<strong>Right — the decisive know-how is tacit.</strong> Lean line-balancing and supplier-quality engineering live in your engineers' hands and routines, not in a manual. That is exactly why 18 months of PDFs moved nothing. Now you can design people-based channels instead of re-shipping documents.`,
                    coach_r1_explicit:      `<strong>You still think this is a documentation problem.</strong> Trimming and re-translating the manuals is what the last 18 months did — and your DPMO barely moved off 14,000. Lean and supplier-quality know-how is tacit; no PDF gets it into a supervisor's hands.`,
                    coach_r1_effort:        `<strong>‘Make them study harder’ misreads the problem.</strong> The supervisors are not lazy — the know-how simply does not transfer through reading. It is tacit, and it moves through demonstration and shared practice, not enforced study time.`,
                    coach_r1_willing:       `<strong>Replacing supervisors is the comfortable, wrong answer.</strong> 70% were promoted internally with no NordHaus-system exposure — that is a missing absorptive base, not resistance. Fire them and you destroy the recipient capacity you need to build, and the family escalates.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> Tacit know-how, and the stickiness sits in the recipient and context — supervisors with no absorptive base across a wide Turkey–Germany distance. That is the lens that tells you to build people-based channels and absorptive capacity, not buy another platform.`,
                    coach_r1_source:        `<strong>Source resistance is a side-issue here.</strong> The engineers are seconded and willing; the real stickiness is the recipient's absorptive capacity and the cultural distance, not the engineers withholding. Retain them, yes — but the barrier is on the receiving side.`,
                    coach_r1_channel:       `<strong>A better platform is not the fix.</strong> A slicker intranet still moves explicit content only. The channel problem is that you used documents for tacit know-how — the answer is people, not a nicer portal.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>That is how you spend the next 12 months like the last 18.</strong> ‘Push harder’ is precisely what failed. There absolutely is a barrier — and it sits in the recipient's capacity and the context, not in repetition.`,
                    coach_r1_lowcandour:    `<strong>Low candour means another documentation project.</strong> If the team will not name this as a tacit-transfer problem, you will run more e-learning and call it a plan — and converge on nothing. Name it honestly.`,
                    coach_r2_overbudget:    `<strong>You have committed roughly €3.6M against a €2.4M budget.</strong> HQ funds convergence, not a shopping list. The overspend buys you nothing — only the affordable money moves know-how, and the over-commit reads to the COO as a team that cannot prioritise. Which two capabilities carry the most EBITDA, and which mechanism is decisive for each?`,
                    coach_r2_docheavy:      `<strong>You have loaded the budget onto documents and the platform.</strong> Cheap, yes — but they move explicit content only. Six months on, your DPMO barely moves. Which engineer's hands is line-balancing in, and how does a portal get it into your supervisors'? Fund the people channel.`,
                    coach_r2_people:        `<strong>People-based mechanisms carry the tacit load.</strong> Inpatriates and joint problem-solving teams move lean and supplier-quality know-how through demonstration and shared practice — the only channels that actually shift tacit capability. Documents and the twin now support, not lead.`,
                    coach_r2_twin:          `<strong>A digital twin supports people; it does not replace them.</strong> You have over-invested in the platform while the people channel is thin. The twin can scaffold a transfer that engineers lead — it cannot lead one.`,
                    coach_r2_overdays:      `<strong>You have committed more engineer-days than exist.</strong> Four seconded engineers do not have unlimited time; over-booking the 600-day pool means the plan cannot be staffed. HQ will not fund a schedule that double-counts the engineers.`,
                    coach_r2_underdays:     `<strong>You funded people channels but starved them of engineer time.</strong> Inpatriation and joint teams need engineer-days to work — without them the money buys intent, not transfer. Match the budget with real engineer time.`,
                    coach_r2_inpat6:        `<strong>Six inpatriates is a big channel — and a heavy production hit.</strong> Pulling six high-potentials off the line at once dents current output and strains the family. A focused cohort moves nearly as much tacit know-how at far less operational cost.`,
                    coach_r2_inpat0:        `<strong>Zero inpatriates leaves no tacit channel home.</strong> Inpatriation is your signature mechanism for moving relational, tacit know-how and seeding it back at Gebze. Send none and you rely on documents for knowledge that documents cannot carry.`,
                    coach_r3_balanced:      `<strong>That is the alignment Kiessling demands.</strong> Standardising the safety and quality gates while adapting features and suppliers to Turkey/MENA aligns HQ intent with local reality — so the transfer you funded actually lifts performance instead of triggering family resistance.`,
                    coach_r3_wholesale:     `<strong>Wholesale standardisation ignores alignment.</strong> Adopting every Stuttgart spec — including suppliers that do not exist in Turkey and features MENA buyers do not want — is exactly the move that makes the family escalate to the board. Kiessling is blunt: transfer without alignment does not lift performance.`,
                    coach_r3_local:         `<strong>Keeping almost everything local forfeits HQ intent.</strong> If you adapt away the quality gates and lean standards, you have alignment with the family but no convergence. Standardise what is non-negotiable; adapt the rest.`,
                    coach_r3_noabsorb:      `<strong>You skipped absorptive capacity.</strong> Supervisors promoted internally with no NordHaus exposure cannot absorb lean systems cold (Cohen & Levinthal). Without bilingual training and a transfer cadence, the know-how arrives and bounces off.`,
                    coach_r3_retain:        `<strong>You retained the engineer who is the channel.</strong> A real role and a transfer-lead mandate keeps your supplier-quality know-how at Gebze — and the balanced alignment means she has scope to apply it. That is protecting the people who are the plan.`,
                    coach_r3_match:         `<strong>Matching salary alone is fragile.</strong> She did not get the counter-offer for money — she wants scope to use what she knows. A pay match with no role change buys a few months, not retention.`,
                    coach_r3_lostengineer:  `<strong>You let the channel walk out.</strong> Your strongest seconded engineer just resigned to the rival, and with her goes the supplier-quality transfer channel — the one capability carrying the most EBITDA. You optimised the plan and forgot the people who <em>are</em> the plan.`,
                    coach_r3_noreintegration: `<strong>You sent inpatriates with nowhere to land.</strong> No role waiting on return means the absorbed know-how leaves with the returnee — your best one to a rival who offered her scope. Inpatriation only pays off with reintegration.`,
                    coach_r4_quarterly:     `<strong>Quarterly milestones give the COO a real plan.</strong> Quarter-by-quarter DPMO and productivity targets, each tied to a mechanism and a capability, are what an accountable convergence plan looks like — not a single year-end promise.`,
                    coach_r4_annual:        `<strong>A single annual target hides the slippage.</strong> The COO cannot tell at month three whether you are on track or repeating the last 18 months. Commit quarterly milestones she can hold you to.`,
                    coach_r4_trust:         `<strong>‘Trust us’ is not a plan.</strong> She has heard it before — from the team she is deciding whether to replace. Without quarterly targets and a causal chain, there is nothing to fund and nothing to hold.`,
                    coach_r4_causal:        `<strong>That is a board-ready case.</strong> Showing each mechanism → absorbed capability → operational metric → ₺ EBITDA proves the plan is not a shopping list but a causal engine for convergence. The COO can fund a chain she can follow.`,
                    coach_r4_financial:     `<strong>The EBITDA number alone is not enough.</strong> The COO funds convergence, but she needs the chain beneath the margin — which mechanism absorbs which capability and moves which metric. A number with no logic reads as a hope.`,
                    coach_r4_activity:      `<strong>An activity list is not a convergence plan.</strong> ‘Training run, manuals shipped, engineers deployed’ is what the failed integration measured. Show outcomes and the causal chain, not the inputs.`,
                    coach_r4_optimistic:    `<strong>Assuming output holds while you pull people is naïve.</strong> Inpatriates and engineers committed to transfer are not also running the line. Without backfill, your current production dips — and the COO will find that assumption first.`,
                    coach_r4_overcommit:    `<strong>Pushing everyone harder collapses the line.</strong> Accepting a production dip to maximise transfer speed is exactly what HQ said you could not do. The plan has to be staffable without breaking current output.`,

